## 4. What I accept, and what it changes

Accepted, and materially useful:

- **§1 — `(D-a)`'s population claim is wrong.** Three populations reach the branch (lapsed /
  cold-but-priceable / cold), not one; TA's complement lapses after `2*DefaultObservationMaxAge`, and
  saturation's tier-1/2 `P0-store` path means a *measured* number often exists for exactly that
  variant. The discriminator being free at the write site — `a.Reason` available because the carrier is
  located **by name with no `Enabled`/`Live` check** — is the part I had not seen, as is the merge
  **discarding** it (`out.Reason` assigned only in the `bByName` hit branch).
- **§2 — sub-case 2 is sizable-but-gated, not unsizable.** That is a real refinement of Finding 44
  rather than a disagreement with it, and it is the sub-case carrying the coder's measured regression
  fixture. Your split into T1-3 (population scope) and T1-4 (`N8`/`VG-up` scope) is the right cut; I
  had them fused.
- **§2(b) — the two narrowings are not the same narrowing.** *"Admit only when saturation votes"* is
  dead by reachability; *"admit only when the carrier has no record"* survives. I will keep them
  visibly separate in the Type 3.
- **§3 / Finding 45** — same sentence, two independent proofs. Repair rests on `:189-192`.
- **§5B T1-9 / T1-10** — new to me; content determined; I will not restate the superseded forms.

**Where my recommendation moves.** I recommended option **3** (defer `(D-a)`, route admission to
`N5`) and I still do — but your §1 changes the *reason*, which matters for how it gets recorded. Not
"defer because the mechanism cannot be made to work" (Finding 44's framing) but "defer because the
true domain is `Reason == no-data`, which is two populations smaller than the frozen text claims, and
the sizable-but-gated population is a separate `N8` question that deferring `(D-a)` does not dispose
of." Recorded as a **decision with a scoped domain**, per your §2 closing — not as an elimination
default.

One addition from the PR-2 reviewer's Finding 47, which arrived alongside yours and bears on T1-2: of
`(D-b)`'s three clamped sites, a tagged variant can reach `costGreedyRolePick` and `fillRole` but
**not** `fairShareRolePick` (empty `AcceleratorName` fails the `available[...]` gate), and `fillRole`
— the unbounded-loop site — has **no** C11 test. So the half of the guard that matters most is the
untested half, and it goes live the moment `(D-a)` does. That is an argument for scoping `(D-a)`
deliberately rather than leaving it dormant-and-unvalidated.

